# 1Password secret references — SAFE TO COMMIT.
#
# This file holds no secrets: every value is a pointer into a vault, resolved at
# run time by `op run --env-file=.env.op -- <command>`. The resolved values live
# in the launched process's memory for its lifetime and nowhere else — not in
# .env.local, not in your shell, not in a dotfile backup.
#
# It is also documentation: it states which secrets this project consumes and
# where they live, which a .env.example can only suggest.
#
# Setup:
#   1. Install the 1Password CLI and enable "Integrate with 1Password CLI" in
#      the desktop app (Settings -> Developer). That is what makes the biometric
#      prompt appear.
#   2. Create the items below in your vault, or edit the references to match the
#      ones you already have. Check a reference with:
#        op read "op://Dev/anthropic-epub-translator/credential"
#
# Adding a secret here is only half the work: the recipe that needs it must be
# wrapped in `op run` in the Justfile. Recipes that make no provider call —
# `estimate`, `glossary-suggest`, `eval-ref` — are deliberately left unwrapped,
# so pricing a book or extracting proper nouns never asks for a credential.

# LLM chat provider (literary mode, style-profile, series-bible).
EPUB_TRANSLATOR_LLM_API_KEY=op://Dev/anthropic-epub-translator/credential

# NMT draft provider. Required by `run`/`batch` when NMT_PROVIDER=google;
# ignored by the self-hosted engines.
EPUB_TRANSLATOR_NMT_API_KEY=op://Dev/gcp-cloud-translation/credential

# A hosted COMET-QE service would go here too — a local one needs no key:
# EPUB_TRANSLATOR_COMET_API_KEY=op://Dev/comet-qe/credential
